::ENG1_U02_L06_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A hero carries fire through a frozen pass to save a village. Adaptation: A student carries a charged radio through a snowstorm so a shelter can call for help. Question: What is preserved?{
~A. The exact object carried by the hero#Incorrect. Fire changes to a radio.
=B. A dangerous journey to bring help to others#Correct. Both characters travel through danger to help a community.
~C. The ancient village setting#Incorrect. The adaptation uses a shelter.
~D. The absence of conflict#Incorrect. Both include danger.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A warrior refuses wise advice before battle. Adaptation: A soccer captain ignores a teammate's strategy before a game. Question: What changed?{
~A. The problem of ignoring advice#Incorrect. That problem remains.
~B. The leader's pride disappears.#Incorrect. Pride remains, so this choice reverses the conflict instead of explaining it.
=C. The setting changes from battle to sports.#Correct. The adaptation modernizes the conflict.
~D. The story becomes a weather report.#Incorrect. It remains a literary situation.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A goddess gives a traveler a map. Adaptation: A librarian gives a student a database guide. Question: What is similar?{
~A. Both texts include a goddess.#Incorrect. The adaptation changes the helper to a librarian.
=B. Both helpers give guidance.#Correct. The form changes, but the helper role remains.
~C. Both texts take place in a library.#Incorrect. Only the adaptation uses a library context.
~D. Both texts remove the journey.#Incorrect. The guide supports a journey or search.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A hero must answer a riddle before entering home. Adaptation: A student must solve a password clue before opening a digital file. Question: How does the adaptation change the source?{
~A. It removes the idea of a test.#Incorrect. The password clue is still a test.
~B. It keeps the exact setting.#Incorrect. Home entry changes to a digital file.
~C. It avoids all comparison.#Incorrect. The question asks for comparison.
=D. It changes a traditional riddle into a modern digital challenge.#Correct. The challenge is updated for a modern context.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A queen protects children during battle. Adaptation: A crossing guard protects students during a traffic emergency. Question: Which theme is preserved?{
=A. Courage can mean protecting vulnerable people.#Correct. Both characters protect children in danger.
~B. Battles and traffic are identical.#Incorrect. They are different situations.
~C. Children should solve every danger alone.#Incorrect. Adults protect them.
~D. Crowns are needed for safety.#Incorrect. The adaptation does not use a crown.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q06::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A hero gives up a crown to rebuild farms. Adaptation: A student gives up a trophy ceremony to restore a school garden. Question: What is preserved?{
~A. The exact royal setting#Incorrect. The adaptation changes royalty to school life.
~B. The presence of burned farms#Incorrect. The adaptation uses a garden.
~C. The absence of sacrifice#Incorrect. Both characters give up a reward.
=D. The choice to serve instead of accept praise#Correct. Both characters give up recognition to help rebuild.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q07::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A mythic river blocks the hero. Adaptation: A difficult final exam blocks the student's graduation goal. Question: What does the adaptation do?{
~A. It removes the obstacle completely.#Incorrect. The exam is still an obstacle.
~B. It keeps the river exactly the same.#Incorrect. The river changes to an exam.
=C. It changes the obstacle from physical to academic.#Correct. The river becomes an exam.
~D. It changes the theme to cooking.#Incorrect. No cooking appears.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q08::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A traveler follows a star through darkness. Adaptation: A student follows emergency lights during a blackout. Question: Why does the adaptation work?{
~A. It removes the guiding light.#Incorrect. Emergency lights still guide.
=B. It preserves guidance through darkness in a modern setting.#Correct. The source idea remains, but the object changes.
~C. It changes the story into nonfiction directions.#Incorrect. It remains an adapted literary situation.
~D. It makes the character ignore all help.#Incorrect. The character follows help.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q09::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A hero's pride causes the first battle to fail. Adaptation: A debate captain's pride causes the team to lose the first round. Question: What theme remains?{
=A. Pride can lead to failure when people ignore others.#Correct. Both versions show pride causing a loss.
~B. Debate has no connection to teamwork.#Incorrect. The adaptation is about a team.
~C. Battles and debates are exactly the same.#Incorrect. They are different contexts.
~D. Pride always guarantees success.#Incorrect. Pride causes failure.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q10::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A hero carries bread across a battlefield. Adaptation: A volunteer carries meals across a flooded street. Question: What changed?{
~A. The act of bringing food to others#Incorrect. That remains in both versions, so it is not the changed part.
~B. The idea of service#Incorrect. Service remains, so this choice does not identify what changed in the adaptation.
=C. The dangerous setting#Correct. The battlefield changes to a flooded street.
~D. The need for courage#Incorrect. Courage remains important.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q11::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A shield with a family symbol gives the hero courage. Adaptation: A jacket with a team patch gives the student courage. Question: What is preserved?{
~A. The exact object is a shield in both texts.#Incorrect. The adaptation uses a jacket.
~B. The family symbol disappears entirely.#Incorrect. It changes to a team patch.
~C. Courage is removed from the adaptation.#Incorrect. Courage remains, so this choice misreads the shared idea between the versions.
=D. An object connected to identity supports courage.#Correct. Both the shield and jacket carry identity meaning.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q12::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: The hero descends into a cave to bring back a lost song. Adaptation: A student searches archived recordings to recover a forgotten school anthem. Question: Which comparison is strongest?{
=A. Both characters search for something culturally meaningful.#Correct. The lost song becomes a forgotten anthem.
~B. Both characters enter a cave.#Incorrect. The adaptation changes the cave to archived recordings.
~C. Both texts are about sports.#Incorrect. Neither version focuses on sports, so this comparison is not supported.
~D. Both characters avoid searching.#Incorrect. Both characters actively search, so this reverses the shared action.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q13::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A messenger runs through smoke to warn the city. Adaptation: A student sends emergency alerts during a lab fire. Question: What is updated?{
~A. The danger disappears.#Incorrect. The lab fire remains dangerous.
=B. The method of warning changes from running to digital alerts.#Correct. The adaptation modernizes communication.
~C. The warning becomes unimportant.#Incorrect. Warning others is central.
~D. The character no longer helps anyone.#Incorrect. The student helps by sending alerts.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q14::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A hero refuses treasure to save the city. Adaptation: A student refuses prize money to fund a class project. Question: What theme is preserved?{
~A. Treasure and prize money are identical.#Incorrect. The objects differ.
~B. Cities and classrooms are always the same.#Incorrect. The setting changes.
=C. Selfless choices can help a community.#Correct. Both give up a reward to serve others.
~D. Rewards should never exist.#Incorrect. The theme is about choosing service over reward.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q15::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A hero receives help from a wise elder. Adaptation: A student receives help from an experienced coach. Question: What stays the same?{
~A. The exact title elder#Incorrect. The adaptation uses a coach.
~B. The ancient setting#Incorrect. The adaptation is modern.
~C. The absence of advice#Incorrect. Advice is present in both.
=D. The helper role#Correct. Both texts include guidance from someone experienced.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q16::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A hero crosses a desert alone. Adaptation: A student crosses an empty campus after a storm. Question: What changed?{
~A. The lonely journey#Incorrect. Both include isolation.
~B. The need for determination#Incorrect. Determination remains.
=C. The harsh setting from desert to campus#Correct. The adaptation changes the environment.
~D. The character's movement#Incorrect. Both characters cross a place.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q17::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A hero lights a beacon for ships. Adaptation: A student turns on a signal light for a rescue team. Question: What is preserved?{
~A. The exact type of ship#Incorrect. The adaptation uses a rescue team.
~B. The ocean setting in both texts#Incorrect. The adaptation changes the context.
~C. The loss of all guidance#Incorrect. Guidance remains central.
=D. A signal light helps guide others to safety.#Correct. Both versions use light as a signal.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q18::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: The hero learns mercy after sparing a defeated enemy. Adaptation: A student learns mercy after forgiving a rival team member. Question: What is preserved?{
=A. A theme about mercy toward a rival#Correct. Both versions keep the mercy theme.
~B. The exact battle scene#Incorrect. The adaptation changes battle to team conflict.
~C. The enemy remains an enemy soldier#Incorrect. The adaptation uses a team member.
~D. The character refuses to change#Incorrect. The character learns mercy.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q19::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: The hero carries a clay bowl of fire. Adaptation: A student carries a charged radio. Question: Why is this a meaningful adaptation?{
~A. The object loses all purpose.#Incorrect. The radio has a purpose.
=B. The helpful object changes to fit the new setting.#Correct. Fire and radio both help a community, but the radio fits the modern shelter.
~C. The source and adaptation are unrelated.#Incorrect. Both involve carrying help.
~D. The student becomes a queen.#Incorrect. That is not stated.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q20::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A hero is tested by a riddle at the gate. Adaptation: A student is tested by a code at the computer lab door. Question: What stays the same?{
~A. The gate remains unchanged.#Incorrect. The adaptation uses a lab door.
=B. A character must solve a challenge to enter.#Correct. Both versions use an entry test.
~C. The riddle remains ancient.#Incorrect. It changes to a code.
~D. The challenge disappears.#Incorrect. The code is still a challenge.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q21::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A village remembers the healer's frozen journey. Adaptation: A school remembers the nurse who walked through snow to bring medicine. Question: Which theme is preserved?{
=A. Communities remember people who serve during hardship.#Correct. Both communities honor service.
~B. Medicine is unimportant.#Incorrect. Medicine matters in both.
~C. Snow removes all conflict.#Incorrect. Snow creates difficulty.
~D. The setting remains a village.#Incorrect. The adaptation changes to a school.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q22::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A warrior's shield carries a moon symbol. Adaptation: A goalkeeper's jersey carries the same moon symbol. Question: What does the adaptation preserve?{
~A. The exact role of warrior#Incorrect. The adaptation changes the role.
~B. The battlefield setting#Incorrect. The adaptation uses sports.
~C. The removal of all symbols#Incorrect. The moon symbol remains.
=D. A symbolic image connected to identity#Correct. The moon symbol remains meaningful.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q23::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A hero returns with healing herbs. Adaptation: A student returns with first-aid supplies. Question: What is changed?{
~A. The helpful return#Incorrect. Both characters return with help.
~B. The need for help#Incorrect. The need for help remains in both versions, so it is not the changed part.
=C. The healing object from herbs to supplies#Correct. The adaptation updates the item while preserving the healing purpose.
~D. The character's success#Incorrect. Both return successfully.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q24::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A mythic bird guides a lost traveler. Adaptation: A drone guides a lost hiker. Question: What is the effect of the adaptation?{
=A. It updates a magical guide into a modern technology guide.#Correct. The guiding role remains but changes form.
~B. It removes guidance.#Incorrect. The drone still guides.
~C. It keeps the mythic bird exactly.#Incorrect. The bird becomes a drone.
~D. It changes the story into a recipe.#Incorrect. It remains a narrative adaptation.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_Q25::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A hero's path becomes a road for others. Adaptation: A student's project becomes a guide for younger classmates. Question: What theme is preserved?{
~A. Roads and projects are identical.#Incorrect. The adaptation changes the image.
~B. Younger classmates cannot learn.#Incorrect. The guide helps them.
~C. The source and adaptation have no connection.#Incorrect. Both show helpful example.
=D. One person's effort can help others follow.#Correct. Both versions show an example becoming guidance.
}
